    title: 'French-law SPA: "represents and warrants" and the triple indemnity verb',
    setup: '<p>A French private equity fund is selling a portfolio company under a French-law SPA to a US strategic buyer. The buyer\'s New York counsel insists on the formula "Seller represents and warrants" for every disclosure clause and a triple "indemnify, defend and hold harmless" indemnification verb. The seller\'s Paris counsel pushes back, saying these formulations are unnecessary under French law.</p>',
    prompt: 'Who is right, and what should the parties actually negotiate?',
    modelAnswer: '<p>Both are partly right. Under French law the representation/warranty distinction is not part of the contract law taxonomy (and the 2016 reform of the Code civil reorganised contractual remedies without adopting Anglo-American labels), so "represents and warrants" is essentially decorative. However, if the SPA is governed by French law but litigated in a forum that may apply local rules of evidence or interpretation, the buyer prefers clear words to anchor its case.</p><p>On indemnification, civil-law systems treat the obligation as a single concept (<em>indemniser</em>); the triple formulation is a US drafting habit. The substantive point the parties should fight over is not the verbs but: (i) whether the buyer must give the seller conduct of any third-party claims (typically yes for tax claims, often no for commercial litigation); (ii) the basket, de minimis and cap; and (iii) the time limits and notification mechanics.</p><p><strong>Compromise:</strong> keep "indemnify" alone and add a stand-alone conduct-of-claims clause; jettison "defend" and "hold harmless" as redundant under French law.</p>',
