    prompt: 'Map the regulatory clearances. For each, identify (i) whether notification is mandatory, (ii) the timetable, (iii) the principal risks, and (iv) what efforts standard the buyer should expect to negotiate in the SPA.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>(1) US HSR — likely notifiable.</strong> Size-of-transaction is well above $133.9m. Size-of-person is comfortably met (buyer is multi-billion-dollar). Mandatory; 30-day initial wait. Risk of Second Request given the AI / pharma overlap — could add 6–9 months. Buyer should expect to argue for "commercially reasonable efforts" with carve-out from any divestiture obligations of the buyer\'s existing pipeline.</p><p><strong>(2) EUMR — primary thresholds:</strong> combined worldwide > €5bn (yes, €7.4bn + €0.07bn), but EU turnover threshold of €250m for each of two parties — only the buyer meets this (€1.2bn); the target is only at €60m. Alternative thresholds: combined worldwide > €2.5bn (yes), but need three Member States with €100m combined turnover and €25m each — almost certainly not met given the small target. Conclusion: very likely no EU dimension. The deal will be reviewed under national merger control regimes where the target has activities — UK certainly, and potentially Germany, France, Italy depending on local revenue distribution.</p><p><strong>(3) UK CMA</strong> — target UK turnover is £40m, below the £100m threshold. Share-of-supply test? Possible if the target has a strong niche position in UK AI-drug-discovery — would need careful analysis. Hybrid threshold? Acquirer would need >33% UK share of supply and £350m UK turnover — probably not. CMA is voluntary in any case; risk is post-completion call-in. Recommendation: voluntary briefing paper to the CMA.</p><p><strong>(4) UK NSI Act — Mandatory notification:</strong> AI is one of the 17 mandatory sectors, the target operates in AI-driven drug discovery, so this is firmly within scope. ISU 30-day initial review, possible 45-day call-in, possible 45-day extension. Risk: non-trivial given the strategic importance of AI to UK industrial policy. Sanctions for non-notification: void transaction, criminal liability. Strongly mandatory; build in 4-month buffer.</p><p><strong>(5) US CFIUS — Mandatory?</strong> Target is non-US so CFIUS applies in respect of US assets / IP only if the target has any. If the target has US-based AI engineers, US-located cloud infrastructure with sensitive data, or US-export-controlled technology, mandatory filing analysis is needed. Otherwise voluntary; risk is post-closing CFIUS review.</p><p><strong>(6) FDA marketing-authorisation transfers and other sectoral approvals:</strong> minor, handled separately.</p><p><strong>Efforts standard recommendation:</strong> For a buyer of this profile (large US strategic with European footprint), expect to negotiate "reasonable best efforts" with explicit carve-outs from any divestiture or behavioural undertaking that would materially impair the strategic value of the deal. The seller will push for "all reasonable efforts" or, in a competitive auction, "best efforts" / hell-or-high-water; expect to compromise on a tiered structure (full hell-or-high-water for non-strategic divestitures up to a defined cap, "reasonable best efforts" beyond that). Long Stop: 12 months from signing, with mutual right to extend 3 months for ongoing regulatory processes. Reverse termination fee: market range 4–6% of equity value for antitrust / FDI failure, lower (or none) for buyer-fault scenarios.</p>',
