    id: 'F-17-01', module: '17', type: 'earn-out-dispute',
    title: 'Earn-out litigation under "commercially reasonable efforts"',
    setup: 'BuyerCo acquired TargetCo from SellerCo eighteen months ago. The SPA included a 24-month earn-out tied to TargetCo achieving £50m of revenue in year two, which would trigger a £15m additional payment. BuyerCo has integrated TargetCo\'s salesforce into BuyerCo\'s broader sales organisation, redirected some sales effort to BuyerCo\'s competing product, and applied BuyerCo\'s accounting policies (more conservative revenue recognition than TargetCo\'s prior policies). TargetCo achieved £42m of revenue. The seller has now alleged that BuyerCo breached the earn-out\'s "commercially reasonable efforts" covenant.',
    prompt: 'Advise BuyerCo on its likely legal exposure and the issues a tribunal would consider. Reference relevant case law.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Key issues.</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Standard of review:</strong> "commercially reasonable efforts" is an objective standard requiring the buyer to act as a reasonable business person would act in the same circumstances.</li><li><strong>Were BuyerCo\'s actions consistent with that standard?</strong> Integration into a broader salesforce can be commercially reasonable if it supports overall growth; redirection to a competing product is more problematic if it diverts resources from the earn-out business; application of more conservative revenue recognition policies depresses reported revenue but is a defensible accounting position.</li><li><strong>Lazard v Qinetiq (Del 2015):</strong> the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing will not give the seller broader rights than negotiated. The express "commercially reasonable efforts" standard governs.</li><li><strong>The tribunal will examine:</strong> business plan at signing; what was reasonably foreseeable; whether the actions were taken in good faith; whether they were consistent with industry practice.</li><li><strong>Defences:</strong> integration synergies justified salesforce reorganisation; redirected effort was responsive to market conditions; accounting policy changes were necessary for consistency.</li><li><strong>Quantification:</strong> even if breach found, the seller must prove that the breach caused the revenue shortfall (causation) — not all of the £8m shortfall may be attributable to the alleged breaches.</li></ol><p><strong>Strategy:</strong> prepare a detailed contemporaneous record; engage industry experts; prepare counter-quantification analysis. Mediation likely beneficial given the difficulty of proving causation precisely.</p>'
