    prompt: 'Identify the principal regulatory and legal frameworks that apply, and outline the structural and procedural steps the parties must take.',
    modelAnswer: '<p><strong>Applicable frameworks:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Williams Act / Securities Exchange Act §14</strong> — the merger requires a proxy filing (Schedule 14A) rather than a tender offer Schedule TO since this is a one-step merger.</li><li><strong>Delaware General Corporation Law (DGCL)</strong> — long-form merger under §251 requires shareholder approval; §262 appraisal rights for dissenting shareholders.</li><li><strong>MFW framework:</strong> because the controlling shareholder (33%) is participating with the buyer, the transaction is a controlling-stockholder transaction. To get business judgment review (vs. entire fairness), the parties must structure ab initio with: (a) an independent special committee with full negotiating authority; (b) a non-waivable majority-of-the-minority shareholder vote. Both must be in place from the very first overture.</li><li><strong>Rule 13e-3 disclosure:</strong> this is a Rule 13e-3 going-private transaction, requiring extensive fairness analysis disclosure.</li><li><strong>Antitrust:</strong> Hart-Scott-Rodino (HSR) filing for the transaction; possibly EU merger control if EU revenue thresholds met.</li><li><strong>US Bankruptcy Code §548 / UVTA fraudulent conveyance considerations:</strong> with $2.5bn of new debt vs. operating cash flows, lenders will require a solvency opinion.</li><li><strong>Financial assistance:</strong> not an issue in Delaware (no equivalent of UK CA 2006 prohibition).</li><li><strong>Special committee process:</strong> independent directors, independent counsel (e.g., Skadden / Wachtell), independent financial advisor, fairness opinion.</li><li><strong>Disclosure-only or substantive litigation:</strong> appraisal arbitrage by hedge funds is a real risk in Delaware go-private transactions; structure to minimise (e.g., agree to certain procedural protections; market check).</li></ol><p><strong>Procedural steps:</strong></p><ol><li>Special committee formed before sponsor contact.</li><li>MFW conditions adopted at outset.</li><li>Merger agreement negotiated by special committee.</li><li>Fairness opinion obtained.</li><li>Schedule 14A and 13E-3 filed.</li><li>Shareholder meeting.</li><li>Merger and re-registration.</li><li>Appraisal proceedings (if any).</li><li>Post-closing tax structuring.</li><li>Debt push-down.</li></ol>'
